## Ported code

`src/petpet_gif/_palette.py`

- The transparent animated GIF technique is a deterministic port of the
  workaround published by **egocarib** in
  <https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/4644> (comment on
  <https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/4640>), and used by
  **camprevail**'s <https://github.com/camprevail/pet-pet-gif> - both MIT
  licensed.
- Changes from the original: no use of `random` (a deterministic free-colour
  search instead) so identical input always produces identical bytes; an
  adaptive palette via `Image.quantize` instead of `convert("P")`, which would
  snap every colour onto Pillow's fixed 216-colour WEB palette; palette parsing
  made defensive against unused slots; helper names prefixed with `_`.
- **Deliberate deviation:** frames are written with disposal method **3**
  ("restore to previous") instead of the original's **2** ("restore to
  background"). Restoring to the background colour is decoded inconsistently -
  FFmpeg paints the transparent region opaque black, Pillow keeps the previous
  frame - which the original gets away with only because its paw sits in the
  same place in every frame. Disposal 3 gives every frame exactly the pixels
  that were rendered for it, in FFmpeg, Pillow and browsers alike, which is what
  lets an overlay move and leave. Verified with `ffmpeg -vsync 0` and with
  Pillow's reader; guarded by `tests/test_transparency.py`.

The squash amplitude constants in `src/petpet_gif/_compose.py`
(`WIDTH_SWING`, `HEIGHT_SWING`, bottom anchor `0.92`) mirror the geometry of
`camprevail/pet-pet-gif` (MIT) so the motion matches the GIFs people recognise.
No code was copied from that repository - the composition code here is written
against its published parameters.
